# 04 - Attacker Box Setup

## Objective

Create a controlled attacker machine to generate suspicious activity for SOC investigation practice.

## VM Specifications

| Setting | Value |
|---|---|
| Name | Attacker-Box |
| RAM | 1024-1536 MB |
| CPU | 1 core |
| Disk | 25 GB |
| Network | Shared Network |
| Hostname | attacker-box |

## Ubuntu Server Installation

Use Ubuntu Server ARM64 for simplicity and resource efficiency.

## Network Verification

```bash
ip addr
```

Record the IP address. Example:

```text
Attacker-Box IP: 192.168.64.12
```

## Install Attack Simulation Tools

```bash
sudo apt update
sudo apt upgrade -y
sudo apt install -y nmap hydra curl wget netcat-openbsd whois dnsutils
```

If hydra is not available, you can simulate SSH brute-force manually.

## Tool List

| Tool | Purpose | Use Case |
|---|---|---|
| nmap | Port scanning | Reconnaissance detection |
| hydra | Password brute-forcing | Authentication attack testing |
| curl | Web requests | Payload delivery simulation |
| netcat | Network connections | Backdoor simulation |
| whois | Domain lookup | OSINT practice |

## Verification

Confirm tools work:

```bash
nmap --version
which hydra
which curl
```

## Responsible Use

All tools in this lab are used exclusively against VMs in the isolated homelab network. No attacks are performed against external systems.

